.TH MKNOD 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mknod \- create a block or character special file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mknod
.I name
.I type
.I major
.I minor
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mknod
creates the device special file
.I name
with
.BR mknod (2).
This is a minimal implementation: it takes the four operands shown
(extra operands are ignored), has no options, and cannot create FIFOs.
.PP
.I type
selects the kind of node; only its first character is examined:
.TP
.B b
Block special file.
.TP
.B c
Character special file.
.PP
.I major
and
.I minor
are decimal numbers, converted with
.BR atoi (3)
and combined into a device number as
.RI ( major " << 8) | " minor .
They are not range-checked.
.PP
The node is created with permissions 0666, reduced by the process
file-creation mask.
The kernel allows only the superuser to create device nodes, and the
filesystem containing
.I name
must support
.BR mknod (2).
Most devices on Substrate appear automatically under
.I /dev
and do not need to be created by hand.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The node was created.
.TP
.B 1
Too few operands, an unknown
.IR type ,
or
.BR mknod (2)
failed.
The usage and unknown-type messages are written to standard output;
the
.BR mknod (2)
error is written to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLE
Create a character device node with major 1, minor 3:
.PP
.EX
# mknod /tmp/null c 1 3
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mkdir (1),
.BR mknod (2),
.BR stat (2)
.SH BUGS
Omitting
.I minor
is not diagnosed and makes
.B mknod
crash.
A
.I minor
greater than 255 overflows into the major number.
